<%# THE ONE CALL SITE, made literally one line at each flow.

    WHY THIS PARTIAL EXISTS. SolanaStudio.walletOps.run() already collapses the
    two transports into one entry point — that is what /tasks/walletops-blocks-
    inline-unification landed. What it does NOT collapse is the four things every
    caller must get right AROUND the call, and each of them has cost a real user
    an entry in this epic:

      1. THE RETURN ADDRESS. redirectLink is where the wallet comes back to. A
         call site that omits it hands the wallet a trip with no way home. That
         defect shipped once and lost an approved entry on QA.
      2. THE APP IDENTITY + CLUSTER. appUrl and cluster are read off the same two
         places on every page, and a site that reads them differently signs
         against a different chain than the one the server prepared for.
      3. THE HANDOFF THAT NEVER HAPPENS. run() ends by handing the OS a universal
         link. If the wallet is not installed, or the user dismisses the app
         switch, THIS document stays alive holding a modal that will never
         resolve and buttons that never come back.
      4. THE TRANSPORT FORK ITSELF. Only 1-3 differ by transport, so the fork is
         real — but it belongs in ONE place, not re-decided at every flow.

    So each flow says WHAT it wants signed and this says HOW a wallet is reached.
    Rendered from layouts/application for the same reason the intents are: the
    inline path resolves on the page that started it, but nothing here may assume
    which page that is.

    NOT A WRAPPER AROUND walletOps' DECISIONS. It chooses no signing method,
    touches no journal, and adds no state. Everything below the modal copy is
    arguments.

    EVERY CONTEST FLOW NOW CALLS THIS. The last to arrive was
    contests/_turf_totals_board, the app's highest-traffic money path: it
    collapsed onto a single walletOps.run in parallel with this partial and kept
    its own redirectLink, appUrl, cluster and pagehide watch inline until
    /tasks/route-board-through-runner routed it here.

    ONE WALLET CALL SITE STILL DOES NOT, and it is worth naming rather than
    leaving to be discovered: the username rename in shared/_alpine_factories
    runs walletOps.run('username_rename') with its own copy of the address book.
    Its card is the engine's leveling modal, not this runner's, so it cannot
    call tmWalletOp as it stands. It does share the handoff watch, through
    window.tmWatchHandoff below, since /tasks/frozen-wallet-overlay-traps-user.
    The address book is the same shape this partial exists to retire, left for
    its own change. %>
<script>
(function () {
  // The redirect return leg is studio-engine's callback view. Named once, here,
  // rather than retyped per flow — see reason 1 above.
  var CALLBACK_PATH = '/auth/phantom/callback';

  // Long enough to outlast the OS app-switch prompt, short enough that a user
  // who dismissed it is not left staring at a dead modal. The value the
  // turf-totals board arrived at by measurement, back when it carried its own
  // copy of this watch.
  var HANDOFF_GRACE_MS = 2500;

  // THE 401 NORMALISATION IS NOT OPTIONAL, and it is why this wrapper exists at
  // all. window.authedFetch answers FALSY on a 401 (having already surfaced the
  // login modal); plain window.fetch answers a 401 Response. A handler that
  // branches on `if (!resp)` therefore reads an expired session as a SUCCESSFUL
  // response unless the fallback speaks the same dialect.
  //
  // AND THE CALLBACK DOCUMENT HAS NO authedFetch. It ships only in the DEFERRED
  // importmap module solana_utils.js, while studio-engine's phantom_callback
  // dispatches from a bare inline script during body parse — so a handler's
  // complete() calling window.authedFetch directly throws a TypeError on the one
  // document that matters, AFTER the user has approved in their wallet and after
  // resume() consumed the journal. That is the original lost-entry incident
  // reproduced at the moment of highest cost.
  //
  // shared/_contest_entry_intent carries its own copy of these nine lines rather
  // than calling this one, deliberately: test/integration/contest_entry_redirect_
  // round_trip_test.rb rebuilds three JS worlds from THAT PARTIAL ALONE, and a
  // cross-partial call would make the entry handlers untestable in that isolation.
  window.tmWalletFetch = function (url, opts) {
    if (typeof window.authedFetch === 'function') return window.authedFetch(url, opts);
    return window.fetch(url, opts).then(function (resp) {
      return resp && resp.status === 401 ? null : resp;
    });
  };

  // THE TWO WIRE CONVERSIONS, in one place. The SERVER speaks base64
  // (serialized_tx out, signed_tx in) and every wallet deeplink protocol speaks
  // base58, so both directions are needed by every intent. The gem's codec owns
  // the base58 half; the chunking on the way back is not optional — 
  // String.fromCharCode.apply blows the argument stack on a full transaction, so
  // the 0x8000 window is what keeps a large contest-create from throwing.
  window.tmWireToBase58 = function (base64) {
    var bytes = Uint8Array.from(atob(base64), function (c) { return c.charCodeAt(0); });
    return window.SolanaStudio.walletTransport.base58.encode(bytes);
  };

  window.tmBase58ToBase64 = function (base58) {
    var bytes = window.SolanaStudio.walletTransport.base58.decode(base58);
    var binary = '';
    var chunk = 0x8000;
    for (var i = 0; i < bytes.length; i += chunk) {
      binary += String.fromCharCode.apply(null, bytes.subarray(i, i + chunk));
    }
    return btoa(binary);
  };

  function solanaModal() {
    try { return window.Alpine && Alpine.store('solanaModal'); } catch (e) { return null; }
  }

  // WHEN THE HOP NEVER HAPPENS. pagehide is the SIGNAL, not the timer: it fires
  // when the app switch DOES take, so its absence after the grace window is what
  // tells us this document is still here. document.hidden covers the iOS case
  // where the switch is in flight but pagehide has not fired yet.
  //
  // THE TWO HALVES ARM AT DIFFERENT MOMENTS, and that is the whole reason this
  // hands back a handle instead of doing both at once.
  //
  //   THE LISTENER arms BEFORE run(), because the navigation it watches for
  //   happens INSIDE run(). Attached afterwards it would be registered after the
  //   event it exists to catch.
  //
  //   THE TIMER arms only once run() has RESOLVED, because that is the first
  //   moment the OS has actually been handed the universal link. runRedirect
  //   AWAITS prepare() and navigates second — so a timer started before the call
  //   is not measuring the app switch at all, it is racing prepare's network
  //   round trips. Contest creation is the worst case: its prepare is a
  //   multipart banner upload plus a rebuild POST, and 2.5s does not cover both
  //   plus an app switch. Two failures fell out of that, and neither is exotic:
  //   a SLOW BUT WORKING create painted "Your wallet app did not open" moments
  //   before Phantom opened, and a create that FAILED had its real error card
  //   overwritten by this one 2.5s later, every single time, so the user never
  //   saw the cause. Raised in review of PR 674.
  //
  // The shape is the one contests/_turf_totals_board arrived at by measurement
  // while it still carried an inline copy (it calls this now): listener before
  // the run, setTimeout after the await.
  //
  // AND WHEN THE USER COMES BACK WITHOUT AN ANSWER (/tasks/frozen-wallet-
  // overlay-traps-user). A hop that TOOK used to end the watch, and that was
  // half of a trap: the user taps, the wallet app opens, they change their mind
  // and come back — and the page comes back with it, still wearing the
  // non-dismissible processing card, scroll-locked, with nothing left watching.
  // So start() also arms the way back, and a return counts only AFTER the
  // handoff: on this transport the wallet's answer lands on the callback page,
  // never here, so a user looking at this document again after the link left is
  // one it will hear nothing more for. Before the handoff that is false —
  // prepare is still running and will navigate — which is why the way back is
  // armed in start() and not beside the pagehide listener.
  //
  //   pageshow, persisted  the page came back out of the bfcache: it was left
  //                        (pagehide) and restored, e.g. a swipe back from the
  //                        wallet's web page or from a callback in this tab.
  //   visible after hidden the page was never unloaded at all. A universal link
  //                        can open the wallet app with this document merely
  //                        hidden behind it, and then nothing is restored, so
  //                        no pageshow fires. Only visibility says they are back.
  //
  // studio-engine's own bfcache cleanup (closeAllDismissible) deliberately KEEPS
  // a dismissible:false card, because on the inline transport the promise behind
  // it is still live. That is right, and it is why the way back belongs here,
  // where the transport is known, rather than in the engine.
  //
  // Exposed as window.tmWatchHandoff for the one caller that cannot use
  // tmWalletOp itself — the username rename, whose card is the engine's
  // leveling modal rather than this runner's.
  function watchHandoff() {
    var left = false;
    var settled = false;
    var onPageHide = function () { left = true; };
    var onPageShow = null;
    var onVisibility = null;
    window.addEventListener('pagehide', onPageHide, { once: true });

    function disarm() {
      window.removeEventListener('pagehide', onPageHide);
      if (onPageShow) window.removeEventListener('pageshow', onPageShow);
      if (onVisibility) document.removeEventListener('visibilitychange', onVisibility);
    }

    return {
      // The handoff took: arm the way back, and count down the grace window.
      // Exactly one of onStranded / onReturned fires, at most once.
      start: function (onStranded, onReturned) {
        // Already away if the switch beat this call — iOS can hide the page
        // before the promise that handed off the link has even resolved.
        var away = left || document.hidden;
        function back() {
          if (settled) return;
          settled = true;
          disarm();
          if (typeof onReturned === 'function') onReturned();
        }
        onPageShow = function (e) { if (e && e.persisted) back(); };
        onVisibility = function () {
          if (document.hidden) { away = true; return; }
          if (away) back();
        };
        window.addEventListener('pageshow', onPageShow);
        document.addEventListener('visibilitychange', onVisibility);

        setTimeout(function () {
          window.removeEventListener('pagehide', onPageHide);
          if (settled) return;
          // The hop took. The way back stays armed for when the user returns.
          if (left || document.hidden) return;
          settled = true;
          disarm();
          onStranded();
        }, HANDOFF_GRACE_MS);
      },
      // The trip never reached the wallet — prepare threw, the journal refused,
      // the wrong account connected. There is no handoff to wait on, so nothing
      // is armed and the listener is released rather than left to accumulate one
      // more dead handler per retry.
      cancel: function () {
        settled = true;
        disarm();
      }
    };
  }
  window.tmWatchHandoff = watchHandoff;

  // ctx  — the intent's own arguments. JSON-serialisable, always: on the
  //        redirect transport this is written to localStorage verbatim.
  // opts — { provider, expectedAccount, preparingTitle, preparingBody,
  //          handoffTitle, handoffBody, onStranded }
  //
  // onStranded means "this page will hear nothing more — hand your controls
  // back". It fires when the hop never happened (the card then says so) AND
  // when the user came back from the wallet without an answer (the card is
  // then retired). A caller answers both the same way: re-enable its buttons.
  //
  // Resolves with the intent's complete() value on the inline transport. On the
  // redirect transport nothing after this resolves in a real browser: the page
  // is gone and the callback finishes the intent by NAME.
  window.tmWalletOp = function (name, ctx, opts) {
    opts = opts || {};
    var provider = opts.provider || window.walletProvider.requireProvider();
    var modal = solanaModal();
    var redirect = provider.transport === 'redirect';

    if (modal) {
      if (redirect) {
        modal.show(opts.handoffTitle || 'Opening Your Wallet',
                   opts.handoffBody || 'Handing this over to your wallet app…');
      } else {
        modal.show(opts.preparingTitle || 'Preparing Transaction',
                   opts.preparingBody || 'Building the transaction — approve it in your wallet when it opens.');
      }
    }

    // Armed here, STARTED after the run — see watchHandoff. An inline transport
    // never navigates, so it gets no watch at all.
    var watch = redirect ? watchHandoff() : null;

    function stranded() {
      var m = solanaModal();
      if (m && m.visible) {
        m.error('Your wallet app did not open. Make sure it is installed on this device, then try again.',
                'Wallet Did Not Open');
      }
      if (typeof opts.onStranded === 'function') opts.onStranded();
    }

    // THE WAY BACK. The user left for the wallet and returned to this page with
    // no answer, which on this transport can only ever arrive elsewhere. The
    // processing card is the trap — non-dismissible, scroll-locked — so it is
    // RETIRED rather than turned into an error: nothing failed, the user simply
    // came back, and they expect the board they left, scrollable and ready to
    // try again. Only a card still processing is this runner's to retire.
    function returned() {
      var m = solanaModal();
      if (m && m.visible && m.state === 'processing') m.close();
      if (typeof opts.onStranded === 'function') opts.onStranded();
    }

    return window.SolanaStudio.walletOps.run(name, ctx, {
      provider: provider,
      expectedAccount: opts.expectedAccount || null,
      appUrl: window.location.origin,
      redirectLink: window.location.origin + CALLBACK_PATH,
      cluster: document.body.dataset.solanaCluster
    }).then(function (result) {
      // THE LINK IS WITH THE OS NOW. Everything before this point was prepare.
      if (watch) watch.start(stranded, returned);
      return result;
    }, function (err) {
      // NOTHING WAS HANDED OFF, so nothing may later claim the wallet failed to
      // open. The caller's catch is about to paint the REAL cause, and a
      // watchdog left running would replace that message 2.5 seconds later —
      // deterministically, not as a race. Rethrown untouched: this branch adds
      // no diagnosis, it only declines to invent one.
      if (watch) watch.cancel();
      throw err;
    });
  };
})();
</script>
